WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:03.407
In this lesson, we'll go through what a knowledge graph means

2
00:00:03.407 --> 00:00:06.810
and how they help represent and retrieve relationships from your data.

3
00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:08.842
You'll then explore the dataset you'll

4
00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.910
work on to build a knowledge graph.

5
00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:14.114
Let's dive in. So, what is a knowledge graph?

6
00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.883
We're going to take a step back for a moment

7
00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:21.354
and reflect on relational databases and what a relational schema looks like.

8
00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:24.691
We all probably are familiar with some scenario like this,

9
00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:26.426
where there'll be some table on the

10
00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.927
left and some table on the right,

11
00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:29.896
and this table in the middle that is

12
00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:32.832
the join table that allows for multiple connections between

13
00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:35.769
the persons and the products in this particular example.

14
00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.772
So let's think about these two tables with the join table.

15
00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:43.043
If you ask a question like, what products has this person purchased?

16
00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.712
You'd first start with the person table and

17
00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:48.381
select this person here, it's identified by abk.

18
00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:51.951
And of course, you do a join over to the Person_Product table.

19
00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:56.089
and you'd see that the Person_Product table has a join between

20
00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:57.657
the abk and some chair, abk

21
00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.692
and a lamp, abk and a desk.

22
00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:03.463
Fantastic. One more join across from the Person

23
00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:05.765
to the Person_Product to the Product table.

24
00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:08.334
And now you've got a complete answer where from

25
00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.837
abk, you can see that here's the products that

26
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:13.440
abk has purchased, this Chair, Lamp and a Desk,

27
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.809
and you can get the details about those purchases.

28
00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:19.179
What if you extended that question? And you

29
00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:21.815
asked who else bought products that ABK bought?

30
00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:23.483
Well, you start with the same

31
00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.785
joins going from ABK to the products,

32
00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:28.221
and then you join back to the Person_Product table,

33
00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.824
and from there, back to the Person table

34
00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:32.659
and now you would get

35
00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:35.161
this person ee who's bought some products

36
00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.963
that abk also bought.

37
00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:40.633
But notice that ee is also bought one extra product.

38
00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.402
Hmm. That's interesting.

39
00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:47.307
So, we could probably extend this one more step and ask, well,

40
00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:50.610
what product should we recommend that abk buy next?

41
00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.880
Now this is kind of the basics of doing a recommendation query.

42
00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:57.150
If you've got some purchase pattern from abk

43
00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.686
that matches a purchase pattern from some other

44
00:01:59.886 --> 00:02:01.821
person or rather a group of people.

45
00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:04.324
The thing you want to recommend to abk is what

46
00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:07.260
that other group of people bought that abk has not.

47
00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:10.897
So the same joins apply from abk through to the products,

48
00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.800
back to the other people that have bought those products.

49
00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:17.070
There's too many joins. It's getting a little confusing.

50
00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:18.738
There is actually a nicer way to

51
00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:20.940
actually think about this and to query this.

52
00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.944
Let's turn this into a graph. The first step is to just

53
00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:26.846
drop all the records that don't matter.

54
00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:29.015
All those gray records that weren't part of the original query,

55
00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:30.650
let's put those aside for the moment and

56
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.819
focus on the records that we know about,

57
00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.521
that are part of the data set so far

58
00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:37.957
that matters for this query about recommending something to abk.

59
00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:39.893
Get rid of all the join table

60
00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:42.028
records in the middle, turn those into arrows,

61
00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:45.365
connecting abk directly over to the products that he's purchased.

62
00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:47.300
And the same thing for ee

63
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.102
over to the products that they've purchased.

64
00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.337
And now we'll just rearrange the records a little bit.

65
00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:54.607
So you've got abk on one side, ee on the other side.

66
00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:56.843
And now it looks much nicer, it's

67
00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:59.646
much easier to understand what's going on. Both abk and ee

68
00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:02.215
are connected to these products in the middle.

69
00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:05.952
but then also this person ee is connected to another product

70
00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.620
that has no connection to abk.

71
00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:10.256
You can start to turn this into a query

72
00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:12.458
by rephrasing it as a pattern match.

73
00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:15.061
Starting with abk on the left side,

74
00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:18.998
you can describe a query in a query language called Cypher

75
00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:20.733
where it looks a little bit like

76
00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.736
SQL, if SQL had pattern matching abilities.

77
00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:25.705
So here we're going to describe a pattern

78
00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:28.708
that describes the data records that you want to find.

79
00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:30.210
We're going to match from a data

80
00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.445
record that we're going to call abk

81
00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.447
that has a label of person

82
00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:36.683
and that has a property with the name

83
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:38.785
key where the value of it is abk.

84
00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:40.753
So that's in round parentheses, we know

85
00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.422
that that's a node in graph terms.

86
00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:44.924
And then we kind of have this

87
00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:48.261
right facing arrow with colon purchased in there.

88
00:03:48.261 --> 00:03:50.530
That's a relationship type that is the purchased.

89
00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:53.700
So match a pattern where abk purchased

90
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.470
over to some other parentheses, we're going to call abkProducts.

91
00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.672
That's going to be a pattern match from abk

92
00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:01.874
to the products that abk matched.

93
00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:04.277
We can return those directly and that's going

94
00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:05.778
to be the products in the middle there.

95
00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:08.881
We can extend that pattern to include

96
00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:11.317
the other people who bought those products.

97
00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:14.220
Since we went from abk to the products that abk purchased,

98
00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:17.523
we can also have relationships going in to those products,

99
00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:20.660
and say, okay, there's some other people who bought those products.

100
00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:23.596
This pattern will find all the products

101
00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.398
that people have in common with abk.

102
00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:26.933
We can extend it a little bit

103
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.702
more to ask that recommendation question problem.

104
00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:33.406
where instead of saying we just want to find these other products,

105
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.276
the other products have a unique property where they're actually

106
00:04:36.743 --> 00:04:38.745
something that abk does not have.

107
00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:40.513
So we have the same pattern match from

108
00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:43.049
ABK to some products, to some other people,

109
00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:45.218
and then extend that to other

110
00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:47.620
products. And now we include a predicate,

111
00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:50.623
saying we actually want a negative pattern to be true,

112
00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:53.493
that abk has not purchased those other products.

113
00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:56.796
So, where not abk purchased those other products.

114
00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:59.999
So down there on the bottom that dotted line from abk,

115
00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:02.335
to the couch, we want that to not exist.

116
00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:05.438
Now we're describing a pattern that both has

117
00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:07.940
records that should exist, something that should not exist.

118
00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.510
The other products now will be reduced

119
00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.845
to only products that abk is not purchased.

120
00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.748
That's what we're going to use as a recommendation that he buys.

121
00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.751
So, what really is a knowledge graph?

122
00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:21.287
It's a kind of database that represents information

123
00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:24.490
as nodes representing things like people, products, blogs,

124
00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.892
whatever you might want to have as

125
00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:28.227
records in a database. But then also

126
00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:32.699
relationships that aren't simply convention of using a join table,

127
00:05:32.699 --> 00:05:34.100
but that are first-class citizen, they're

128
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.436
actually data records inside of the database

129
00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:38.137
that have a semantic meaning that they

130
00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:39.806
actually represent how two nodes are connected.

131
00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:42.875
And they add information about those two nodes.

132
00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.877
So both the nodes and relationships have key

133
00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:47.513
value properties, they have multiple labels on nodes.

134
00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:51.417
Relationships always have a direction, and they have a single type.

135
00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.952
And the query language that you use

136
00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:55.154
for doing pattern matching is called Cypher.

137
00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:56.489
And it turns out that this is

138
00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:58.591
super nice for mapping to natural language.

139
00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:01.828
and really convenient for working with GenAI and LLMs

140
00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:03.996
because LLMs are really good at natural language.

141
00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:06.299
If we go back for a moment here,

142
00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.234
and just look at that query at the top,

143
00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.737
you can read that out loud and make sense of it.

144
00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:14.507
Match abk who's a person whose name is abk

145
00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:16.743
that bought some products that some other

146
00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:19.245
people have bought, they've bought some other products

147
00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:22.115
where abk has not purchased those other products.

148
00:06:22.582 --> 00:06:24.751
The other thing that's interesting about knowledge graphs

149
00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:28.121
is that they're really convenient for combining unstructured data along with that

150
00:06:28.121 --> 00:06:31.457
structured data, whether that was some

151
00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:32.725
products that we have or people,

152
00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:34.527
whatever it might be in structured data,

153
00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:36.562
you can then add any kind of chunked text,

154
00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:38.164
do a vector representation of that,

155
00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:39.866
store that in the database as well,

156
00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:43.703
and combine both vector similarity search and pattern matching together

157
00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:46.205
for all kinds of powerful access patterns.

158
00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:49.375
So for example, if you want to do root-cause analysis

159
00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.078
and you imagine that you're a furniture manufacturer

160
00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:55.348
looking to understand customer complaints about your products.

161
00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:58.551
So you turn to your team of experts, you know, data engineering,

162
00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:01.788
analysis team and say, hey, could you do some root-cause analysis?

163
00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:04.424
So I've heard a lot of complaints out on the internet

164
00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:07.260
about our products. Let's try to figure out what's going on.

165
00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.962
So the root-cause analysis would include questions like,

166
00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:13.566
which products have the most issues? And then based on those products,

167
00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:15.768
well, what part of the product is the actual problem?

168
00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:17.737
And then you might want to ask a question like,

169
00:07:17.737 --> 00:07:19.472
is there a problem with the part itself?

170
00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:20.773
Perhaps there are other things that are

171
00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:22.675
going on. People just don't like the product.

172
00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:24.811
But if you can take corrective measures, it would

173
00:07:24.811 --> 00:07:26.245
be because there's something either in

174
00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:28.448
the manufacturing process, which you're looking for

175
00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:30.483
here is for corrective measures that you can take.

176
00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:33.386
do root-cause analysis for something that you can improve.

177
00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:35.154
So if it's a design problem, you

178
00:07:35.154 --> 00:07:36.556
could turn that over to the design team.

179
00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:39.091
They could take a look at what people's complaints are about.

180
00:07:39.358 --> 00:07:41.194
But if it's a manufacturing problem

181
00:07:41.194 --> 00:07:42.895
or a problem with a part somewhere,

182
00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:46.199
you can do some analysis on that, find the part problem

183
00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:48.301
and either swap out the part for a new

184
00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:50.470
one or figure out how to improve the process itself.

185
00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:53.673
So as a data engineer, if you're handed this task,

186
00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:56.843
you would start with the usual thing, ask some clarifying questions,

187
00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:59.111
Why are we really doing this.

188
00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:00.513
You want to be able to understand that

189
00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:04.050
there's possible manufacturing problems in the supply chain. Cool. Okay.

190
00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:07.620
Second question is or if that's what we want to find out,

191
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.922
what data is available that we can do this analysis with?

192
00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:11.891
So it turns out in this

193
00:08:11.891 --> 00:08:13.626
scenario, we've got a bill of materials,

194
00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:16.195
some CSV files that connect the product

195
00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:18.431
all the way to the suppliers from those products.

196
00:08:18.431 --> 00:08:21.067
Maybe they came from some spreadsheets, maybe it came from some

197
00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.136
relational database that isn't really suited for doing analysis.

198
00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:27.273
That's okay too. And you also have some data from

199
00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:30.176
user reviews that you scraped from some websites on the internet.

200
00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:32.445
You want to be able to combine those into one

201
00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.780
dataset that you can then do some analysis on.

202
00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:36.649
So, to do that analysis, you're going to

203
00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:38.584
build a knowledge graph that connects all the data.

204
00:08:39.218 --> 00:08:41.787
So here's what the target data model is going to look like.

205
00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:44.323
On the left, in the sort of beige boxes

206
00:08:44.323 --> 00:08:46.993
over there, you see the CSV files that are available.

207
00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:48.561
Each one of those is going to turn into

208
00:08:48.561 --> 00:08:51.397
nodes in the graph or relationships in the graph.

209
00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:53.499
That's going to be the kind of

210
00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:55.067
what we're going to call the domain graph.

211
00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:57.270
On the right hand side, on the bottom

212
00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:01.007
there you'll also see some beige boxes that represents markdown files.

213
00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.075
Those markdown files are going to be chunked up, they're

214
00:09:03.075 --> 00:09:05.511
going to create some documents inside of the graph as well.

215
00:09:05.978 --> 00:09:08.014
And then all of this is going to get connected.

216
00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:09.515
We'll look at some of the details as we

217
00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:12.251
actually go through doing the the agent descriptions themselves.

218
00:09:12.518 --> 00:09:14.887
But what I want to call out here for you right now,

219
00:09:15.087 --> 00:09:17.490
is that these are really very connected,

220
00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.725
but also very distinct parts of the graph.

221
00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:21.561
We've got the data sources, the

222
00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:23.563
structured data on the one side there.

223
00:09:23.863 --> 00:09:26.566
That's going to turn into what we call the domain graph.

224
00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:28.167
So that's the structured data that we

225
00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:30.202
know that we can query with pattern matching.

226
00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:33.172
On the other side, the unstructured data sources

227
00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:34.974
are not going to be queried in the same way,

228
00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:37.577
and they're not going to be imported in the same way either.

229
00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:40.446
So, they're going to turn into what we call the Lexical Graph,

230
00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:42.815
which represents the original textual data,

231
00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:45.718
plus also the structure around that textual data.

232
00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:47.286
To connect those two pieces,

233
00:09:47.286 --> 00:09:49.388
the unstructured data and the structured data,

234
00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:51.290
we've got this part in the middle

235
00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.959
that we're going to call the Subject Graph.

236
00:09:53.559 --> 00:09:55.661
The subject graph is going to be subjects where

237
00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.930
entities that we pull out of the chunks, so the

238
00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.366
chunks will have some text that will be talking about products,

239
00:10:00.366 --> 00:10:02.535
maybe they'll be user names and things.

240
00:10:02.535 --> 00:10:04.236
All of those things might be entities.

241
00:10:04.637 --> 00:10:07.106
We're going to find those, call those things subjects.

242
00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:08.507
And those subjects are going to be connected

243
00:10:08.507 --> 00:10:10.843
to objects, so the subjects talk about something.

244
00:10:10.843 --> 00:10:14.814
So, some user, let's say that it's me, user abk,

245
00:10:14.814 --> 00:10:16.716
loves this table.

246
00:10:16.716 --> 00:10:20.820
And so there'll be a subject predicate object in the subject graph

247
00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.922
that talks about abk loving some table.

248
00:10:23.489 --> 00:10:25.758
That also, because abk loves the

249
00:10:25.758 --> 00:10:28.561
table, the table can correspond with the product

250
00:10:28.561 --> 00:10:30.596
that we have in the structured data.

251
00:10:30.596 --> 00:10:32.732
If abk mentions is a table and the table

252
00:10:32.732 --> 00:10:34.834
